Let's understand the meanings of the parts of this equation.

$\begin{align}
&\boxed{\begin{align}
\scriptsize\text{Josiah's speed in}&\qquad\scriptsize\text{Number of seconds}
\\
\scriptsize\text{meters per second}&\quad\qquad\scriptsize\text{Josiah skated}
\\
&\searrow\quad\qquad\swarrow
\\
&\large \underbrace{\overbrace{2}\cdot\overbrace{x}}
\\
&\qquad\uparrow
\\
&\scriptsize\text{Distance Josiah skated}
\end{align}}
\\\\
&\boxed{\begin{align}
&\quad\;\;\large \underbrace{10}
\\
&\qquad\uparrow
\\
&\scriptsize\text{Josiah's head start}
\end{align}}
\end{align}$

So $10+2x$ is Josiah's total distance from the starting line, and so is $y$.
